/T (Industry Overview    )
/E (Industry Overview    )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
427 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 260 0 R
/E (Canada's domestic air travel market has fully recovered from the pandemic, with passenger numbers once again reaching record highs. The market is dominated by two large national carriers, while a rotating group of smaller and low-cost airlines competes for price-sensitive travellers. Over the past decade, several newer carriers have entered the market with ambitious expansion plans, only to scale back or shut down within a few years.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
428 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 260 0 R
/E (Running an airline in Canada comes with unique challenges. Distances between cities are long, and many communities are small, making it difficult to fill aircraft on every route. Harsh winters bring de-icing, delays, and cancellations, particularly in Atlantic Canada and the North. Airport fees in Canada are among the highest in the world, and airlines must lease gates at each airport they serve, whether or not those gates are busy.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
429 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 260 0 R
/E (On top of this, airlines face rising costs across the board. Jet fuel prices remain volatile, a national pilot shortage has pushed up crew wages, and passenger protection rules require airlines to compensate or refund travellers when flights are significantly delayed or cancelled. In this environment, it is crucial for airlines to know where they make money, and where they do not.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
