450 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (NorthByte's fiscal year runs from September 1 to August 31. On paper, the fiscal year that just ended, FY2025-26, should have been NorthByte's best yet. Total revenue grew by about 5% compared to FY2024-25, driven by more passengers and the biggest expansion in NorthByte's recent history. In spring 2025, NorthByte launched three new connections and grew its schedule to carry more travellers than ever. The expansion was celebrated internally as a major milestone, adding new cities \(London and Moncton\) to NorthByte's map for the first time in five years.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
451 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (However, the growth did not reach the bottom line. Over the same period, NorthByte's costs grew faster than its revenue. NorthByte's flights were profitable through the fall of FY2024-25, earning a margin of roughly 1% to 10% each month, but they have lost money every month since February 2025. In FY2025-26, NorthByte lost about $40 million on its flights, roughly four times its loss the year before. That figure does not yet include what NorthByte pays to lease its airport gates.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
452 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (NorthByte's leadership knows that something needs to change, but company-wide reports have not made it clear where the money is being lost. Fuel and labour costs have both risen, winter disruptions have increased refunds, and competitors continue to invest in their loyalty programs. Before committing its limited budget, NorthByte wants an independent, data-driven view of what is really behind its losses.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
